    <h2>PHASE 2 - TRANSMITTER INSTALLATION</h2>

    <h3>3.2 TRANSMITTER MOUNTING</h3>
    <p>Orient the process flanges to enable process connections to be made.</p>
    <p>It is important to mount the transmitter and to lay the process piping so that gas bubbles, when measuring liquids, or condensate when measuring gases, can flow back into the process.</p>
    <p>The vent/drain screw valves have to be ordered higher than the taps on liquid service in order to allow the venting of entrapped gas or below the taps on gas service in order to allow the drain of condensate.</p>
    <p>For safety reasons, orient the drain/vent valves so that process fluid is directed down and away from technicians when the valve is used. This can be accomplished by pointing the hole in the outside valve body downward and away. In addition, consider the need for a testing or calibration input, and provide space for the housing covers to be removed for electrical wiring and maintenance.</p>

    <div class="warning">
        <strong>WARNING !</strong>
        <p>Process leaks may cause harm or result in death.</p>
        <p>Install and tighten process connectors and all accessories (including manifolds) before applying pressure.</p>
        <p>In case of toxic or otherwise dangerous process fluid, take any precautions as recommended in the relevant Material Safety Data Sheet when draining or venting.</p>
        <p>Use only a 12 mm (15/32 ") hexagonal spanner to tighten the bracket bolts.</p>
    </div>

    <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-around; align-items: flex-start;">
        <div>
            <img src="placeholder_image_1.png" alt="Transmitter Mounting Diagram 1" style="max-width: 45%;">
            <p class="figure-caption">Traditional (Barrel) housing vertical mounting with bracket for vertical (option B1 and B2).</p>
        </div>
        <div>
            <img src="placeholder_image_2.png" alt="Transmitter Mounting Diagram 2" style="max-width: 45%;">
            <p class="figure-caption">Traditional (Barrel) housing direct mounting for vertical or horizontal connections</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
    <h2>PHASE 2 - TRANSMITTER INSTALLATION</h2>

    <h3>3.3 ROTATION</h3>
    <p>To improve field access to wiring or the readability of the optional LCD meter it is possible to rotate the Housing and the Meters or the Integral display.</p>

    <h4>Housing Rotation</h4>
    <ol>
        <li>Unlock the housing rotation set screw by turning it 1 turn (use the 3 mm Allen key supplied with the instrument).</li>
        <li>Turn the housing clock wise or counterclockwise up to 180° from its original position.</li>
        <li>Caution: over rotating will damage the transmitter.</li>
        <li>Tighten the set screw.</li>
    </ol>

    <h4>Output Meters rotation</h4>
    <p>In case an optional meter is installed, it is also possible to rotate the meter 90° degree clockwise or 270° counterclockwise with 15° steps.</p>
    <p>Just turn it in the required position gripping around the complete meter to avoid pulling the cover from the base of the meter.</p>

    <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-around; align-items: center; margin-top: 30px;">
        <img src="placeholder_image_3.png" alt="Housing Rotation Diagram" style="max-width: 45%;">
        <img src="placeholder_image_4.png" alt="Output Meter Rotation Diagram" style="max-width: 45%;">
    </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
    <h2>PHASE 3 - TRANSMITTER WIRING</h2>

    <h3>4.1 PROTECTIVE GROUNDING</h3>
    <p>All transmitters are supplied with an external ground connection for protective grounding.</p>
    <p>Wire the ground connection marked with <span data-field="grounding_symbol">{{ grounding_symbol }}</span> to a suitable earth ground.</p>
    <p>For a transmitter measuring loop an earth ground should maintain a resistance of 5 ohms or less.</p>
    <p>Use a heavy conductor, at least 15 AWG / 1.6 mm² Ø</p>

    <div class="warning">
        <strong>WARNING !</strong> - A protective grounding connection is absolutely necessary to insure personnel protection, to protect against surge (in case of installation of this option) and to prevent explosions in potentially explosive environment.
    </div>

    <div class="warning">
        <strong>WARNING !</strong> - In case the surge protection option is present and the transmitter is installed in a Hazardous Area, the surge protection must be power supplied voltage source isolated from mains (galvanic separation). Furthermore, the potential equalization for the entire powering cable must be guaranteed since the intrinsic safety circuit of the transmitter is grounded.
    </div>

    <h3>4.2 ELECTRICAL CONNECTIONS</h3>
    <p><strong>WARNING !</strong> - Do not make electrical connections unless the electrical code designation stamped on the transmitter data plate agrees with the classification of the area in which the transmitter is to be installed. Failure to comply with this warning can result in fire or explosion.</p>
    <p>The 4 to 20 mA dc output signal and the dc power supply to the transmitter are carried from the same pair of wires. The supply voltage at the transmitter terminals must be between the limits of 10.5 and 42V dc.</p>
    <p>For Ex ia and intrinsically safe (FM, CSA and AAA) approval power supply must not exceed 30 Vdc. In some countries the maximum power supply voltage is allowed to be voltage limited.</p>

    <div class="warning">
        <strong>WARNING !</strong> - Electrical shock can result in death or serious injury. Avoid contact with the leads and terminals. High voltage that may be present on leads can cause electrical shock.
    </div>

    <p>Follow these steps to wire the transmitter:</p>
    <ol>
        <li>Remove the temporary plastic cap from one of the two electrical connection ports located at both sides in the rear of the transmitter housing.</li>
        <li>These connection ports have a 1/2 inch internal NPT threads. Various adaptors and bushings can be fitted to these threads to comply with plant wiring (conduct) standards.</li>
        <li>Remove the housing cover of the "field terminals" side. See the indication on the label on top of the housing.</li>
        <li>Connect the power supply to the transmitter. Do not remove the transmitter covers when power is applied to the unit.</li>
        <li>In case an Output meter is present it has to be removed by pulling it out. Grip around the complete meter to avoid pulling the cover from the base of the meter.</li>
    </ol>

    <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-around; align-items: center; margin-top: 30px;">
        <img src="placeholder_image_5.png" alt="Wiring Diagram 1" style="max-width: 45%;">
        <img src="placeholder_image_6.png" alt="Wiring Diagram 2" style="max-width: 45%;">
    </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
    <h2>PHASE 4 - TRANSMITTER OPERATION</h2>

    <h3>5. TRANSMITTER OPERATION</h3>
    <p>Use the following step to adjust the transmitter zero.</p>

    <h4>5.1 PRESSURE MEASUREMENT</h4>
    <ol>
        <li>Slowly open the gate valve to admit process fluid to primary of side H.</li>
        <li>Vent all entrapped air (liquid service) or drain any condensate (gas service) from primary using the vent/drain valve.</li>
        <li>Close the gate valve.</li>
        <li>Bring the process to the desired zero reading condition, i.e.</li>
        <li>As an example:</li>
        <ul>
            <li>In case of dry leg open the drain valve to bring the primary to atmospheric pressure</li>
            <li>In case of wet leg slowly open the filling tee (and make sure to have the wet leg completely filled-in).</li>
        </ul>
        <li>The output should be 4 mA. If not:</li>
        <ul>
            <li>Rotate the namplate to get access to the external push buttons.</li>
            <li>Push the zero (Z) button on top of the transmitter (fig. 5.2) for at least 2 seconds.</li>
            <li>The output goes to 4 mA, and if present the integral display, the message "ZERO PASS" will appear.</li>
            <li>If the message "ZERO PASS" does not appear, check the dip switches settings (you need to open the cover on the electronic side and to pull out the integral display; see fig. 5.1).</li>
            <li>In case of other diagnostic messages, see the reference manual.</li>
            <li>Close any open filling tee and drain valve.</li>
        </ul>
    </ol>

    <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-around; align-items: center; margin-top: 30px;">
        <img src="placeholder_image_7.png" alt="Dip Switches Setting Diagram" style="max-width: 45%;">
        <img src="placeholder_image_8.png" alt="Zero Button Diagram" style="max-width: 45%;">
    </div>

    <h4>5.2 LEVEL MEASUREMENT</h4>
    <ol>
        <li>Vent all entrapped air from primary using vent/drain valves on the transmitter, then close them.</li>
        <li>Make sure to have the level in the tank at the required reference (minimum) level.</li>
        <li>Note: In case it is not possible to empty the tank, it is possible to follow the Zero Raise/Lower adjustment procedure described in the reference manual.</li>
        <li>After venting, the output should be 4 mA dc. If not:</li>
        <ul>
            <li>Rotate the namplate to get access to the external push buttons.</li>
            <li>Push the zero (Z) button on top of the transmitter (fig. 5.2) for at least 2 seconds.</li>
            <li>The output goes to 4 mA, and if present the integral display, the message "ZERO PASS" will appear.</li>
            <li>If the message "ZERO PASS" does not appear, check the dip switches settings (you need to open the cover on the electronic side and to pull out the integral display; see fig. 5.1).</li>
            <li>In case of other diagnostic messages, see the reference manual.</li>
        </ul>
    </ol>

    <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-around; align-items: center; margin-top: 30px;">
        <img src="placeholder_image_9.png" alt="Level Measurement Diagram 1" style="max-width: 45%;">
        <img src="placeholder_image_10.png" alt="Level Measurement Diagram 2" style="max-width: 45%;">
    </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
    <h3>DISMANTLING AND REASSEMBLY</h3>

    <h4>Required tools</h4>
    <ul>
        <li>2 mm Allen key</li>
        <li>3 mm Allen key</li>
        <li>Small Phillips screwdriver</li>
        <li>Small flat-bladed screwdriver</li>
        <li>13 mm spanner</li>
        <li>Triangular wrench - (Range > 17 Nm - 12.6 foot lbs)</li>
    </ul>

    <h4>Dismantling</h4>
    <ol>
        <li>Screw down completely the cover locking screw, electronics side, using the 3 mm Allen key.</li>
        <li>Unscrew and remove the covers.</li>
        <li>Unscrew the two fixing screws and remove the secondary electronic assembly.</li>
        <li>Remove the tang grub screw using the 2 mm Allen key and remove the tang.</li>
        <li>In case of an Output meter is present it has to be removed by pulling it out. Grip around the complete meter to avoid pulling the cover from the base of the meter.</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="caution">
        <strong>CAUTION</strong> - Dismantling and reassembly should not be carried out on site because of the risk of damage to the transmitter and its peripherals. All dismantling and reassembly procedures given below should be carried out in the listed order to avoid instrument damage.
    </div>

    <h4>Reassembly</h4>
    <div class="warning">
        <strong>WARNING</strong> - Assembling flanges with incorrect fixing bolts and nuts and improper "O" rings can cause fracture or overstressing of bolts and release of pressurized process fluid. Only use the supplied gaskets and O-rings and ensure that the specified torque limits are not exceeded. Do NOT REMOVE the O-ring fitted in the sensor neck: it provides the housing a degree of protection.
    </div>
    <ol>
        <li>Insert the sensor cable in its recess at the bottom of the housing.</li>
        <li>Screw the housing down completely until the nesting of the sensor cable is achieved. If the sensor cable is not in the desired position and lock it with the tang grub screw using the 2 mm Allen key.</li>
        <li>Plug the sensor cable to the secondary electronics. Fix the secondary electronics to the housing.</li>
        <li>Refit the covers and tighten securely.</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="warning">
        <strong>WARNING</strong> - For Hazardous Location installations, at least eight (8) threads on the cover must be engaged in order to meet the flameproof (explosion-proof) requirements.
    </div>

    <p>Unscrew the cover locking screw to secure the covers. This is mandatory to meet "Flameproof requirements" for Hazardous Areas installation.</p>

    <h4>PRESSURE TEST WIRING</h4>
    <p>Once reassembled the process flanges and the transmitter, a pressure test is required. At this purpose, apply a hydrostatic pressure to the process flange and the transmitter, and hold it for one minute then verify that no leakages occurred, otherwise repeat the assembly procedure and the pressure test.</p>

    <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-around; align-items: center; margin-top: 30px;">
        <img src="placeholder_image_11.png" alt="Transmitter Sectional View" style="max-width: 45%;">
    </div>
</div>

